content.text:
{
  "rows": [
    {
      "correct_choice": "E",
      "explanation_id": "87314432-5373-49d3-aed5-e5609240bc6a",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Whether or not Erich can be convinced is not discussed in Leslie’s argument.",
          "id": "0fb60778-31be-4320-9bb7-ac90683a879e",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "13c9f980b2582437a4ac64afb20c4a85"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is evidence that supports the argument’s conclusion.",
          "id": "51ad116e-0552-4387-b559-8889eaf965a9",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "1a36f9bede56445bb0d06bb4bbf743a5"
        },
        {
          "body": "Leslie’s argument assumes that Erich should not risk his physical well-being for the treasure, but this goes further and says that Erich should not risk his physical well-being for any possible gain.",
          "id": "60fd84eb-ab5a-4b33-8ad5-2ebdd71dbb31",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "051d59663a1e97bfb38787a98d5ce300"
        },
        {
          "body": "This best paraphrases Leslie’s conclusion.",
          "id": "8b4f5809-79a1-4fd9-8ef7-379e2edd2717",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "21423a0340558c03b57e0a7089e025ed"
        },
        {
          "body": "Leslie does not assert that the treasure has no value to him.",
          "id": "e723c5e7-00a2-4ca0-b1c3-9f342889c10e",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "58432e0d1cfd1d939dac9d49af7ecdd6"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "Which one of the following most accurately expresses the main conclusion drawn in Leslie's argument?",
      "qid": "PT127-S3-Q19",
      "question_type": "Main Conclusion",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "Your quest for the treasure is irrational.",
          "id": "1adc2fd8-ae30-4fc3-af67-b388f6c3cf81",
          "label": "Conclusion",
          "md5": "98865faf06b64b51eb3c8db69a9a6ce9",
          "position": 1
        },
        {
          "body": "You would not sacrifice one of your hands in order to possess the world. Your hands are more important than possessing the world. Your entire body is necessarily more important to you than your hands. You are ruining your health and harming your body in your quest for a treasure that is much less valuable than the whole world.",
          "id": "fba40d36-8ba9-4dcc-8ed8-7e543a741f39",
          "label": "Evidence",
          "md5": "f44c05921b161ebade451b18f188c40c",
          "position": 0
        }
      ],
      "stimulus": "Leslie: I'll show you that your quest for the treasure is irrational. Suppose you found a tablet inscribed, \"Whoever touches this tablet will lose a hand, yet will possess the world.\" Would you touch it? Erich: Certainly not. Leslie: Just as I expected! It is clear from your answer that your hands are more important to you than possessing the world. But your entire body is necessarily more important to you than your hands. Yet you are ruining your health and harming your body in your quest for a treasure that is much less valuable than the whole world. I rest my case."
    },
    {
      "correct_choice": "C",
      "explanation_id": "7aca97bc-3629-4277-bbd4-5597bdda2c4c",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "B also takes road 1. The warmer discharge sends bass off to find cooler water elsewhere. Fewer bass stay downstream of the plant, so anglers there catch fewer. That explains the drop, so eliminate B.",
          "id": "09715b96-d8dc-43ac-9da7-8df2738d6c9d",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "de9dccba80e97678b0157e78e65301df"
        },
        {
          "body": "E is road 1 again. Turbulence from the wastewater disrupted the river vegetation and destroyed some of the bass's habitat. Less habitat supports fewer bass downstream, so fewer get caught. E explains the drop.",
          "id": "6b186f39-db7c-4cdb-811b-9d07def23aa5",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "6a6bee81d2049649f7022372e5ae523a"
        },
        {
          "body": "D is the road 2 answer, and it is easy to throw out as off topic because it is about anglers, not bass. The bass may be fine. But noise makes that stretch of river a less popular place to fish, and fewer anglers catch fewer bass. That is exactly the decrease described, so D explains it and is out.",
          "id": "96597a73-05b1-4189-b7a8-85bb2b514718",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "7dfe71e3bac8d83ea895f992cbb4b2bf"
        },
        {
          "body": "C pushes the other way. Better access brings more anglers fishing for bass downstream. More people fishing should mean more bass caught, not fewer, so C cannot explain the decrease.\n\nCould you rescue C by imagining the extra anglers fished the bass out? Only by inventing that story yourself, and it runs against what C actually tells us. C's own effect is more people fishing, and on its face that means more bass caught. The four other answers each point straight at fewer bass caught; C points the other way. That is what marks the answer that does not belong.",
          "id": "a7363b92-0e04-4d9f-92f5-559a26c2300a",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "4d3c4a7cdad4aef9177a0cb919ffc800"
        },
        {
          "body": "A takes road 1. If the wastewater draws in fish that prey on bass, more bass get eaten and fewer are left downstream to catch. That explains the drop in bass caught, so A is out.",
          "id": "bc1e9cd3-871c-463a-ae9e-14108e66a53e",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "516909251cc4c1b0272980e5f0ced573"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "Each of the following, if true, could explain the decrease described above EXCEPT:",
      "qid": "PT108-S3-Q6",
      "question_type": "Paradox EXCEPT",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "The power plant opened and started discharging wastewater into the river.",
          "id": "020f306b-a3e9-4046-8b8f-8e2726f19554",
          "label": "Given that ...",
          "md5": "c0d3870b52f4e43398903b56aedb04c7",
          "position": 0
        },
        {
          "body": "This isn't a true paradox. Nothing here is surprising. The stem just wants explanations for a drop in the number of bass caught.\r\n\r\nThink about what \"number of bass caught\" depends on. It takes two things: bass in the water and people fishing for them. So there are two roads to fewer bass caught:\r\n1. **Fewer bass downstream.** They died, got eaten, moved away, or lost their habitat.\r\n2. **Less fishing downstream.** Fewer anglers, or anglers fishing less.\r\n\r\nExpect most of the answers we eliminate to come in on road 1, with at least one sneaking in on road 2.\r\n\r\nMost students only think of road 1, because the wastewater feels like it must be hurting the fish. But road 2 is just as good. If half the anglers stop showing up, half as many bass get caught, even if the bass are thriving.",
          "id": "30050c0b-3c74-407f-a7ab-42bd91a5178d",
          "label": "Evaluate",
          "md5": "c9fe331374bbbe17c7ac3f9ed1a95db6",
          "position": 2
        },
        {
          "body": "Eliminate four answers that explain fewer bass caught, by either road. The correct answer will do neither, or it will push the other way: more bass or more fishing, which should mean *more* bass caught.\r\n\r\nBe careful with any answer about anglers. It isn't out of scope. Anglers are half the equation.",
          "id": "3a915989-8077-4179-8156-11839c6a5952",
          "label": "Goal",
          "md5": "3c26722b61516a64cf1806abf64423e2",
          "position": 3
        },
        {
          "body": "Shortly after, anglers downstream caught fewer bass overall?",
          "id": "78fa0921-1787-4fe5-870a-f74547986e47",
          "label": "Why is it that ...",
          "md5": "27aa4f4da5027ff65c59223ce33f874d",
          "position": 1
        }
      ],
      "stimulus": "Shortly after the power plant opened and began discharging its wastewater into the river, there was a decrease in the overall number of bass caught by anglers downstream from the plant."
    },
    {
      "correct_choice": "E",
      "explanation_id": "ac2194b1-6d03-4995-b8f9-556aa1f12fa4",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "This compares sewage pollution with pollution \"in some other way.\" The stimulus never mentions any other kind of pollution, so we have nothing to compare against. Even for sewage, the fish kill only happens \"sometimes,\" so we can't rank sewage as the deadlier pollutant.",
          "id": "1ba9f93a-e161-4092-aa9b-4847ae6d45ae",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "0791ce133cf77d96687d93891cff7b33"
        },
        {
          "body": "The stimulus describes one way sewage can harm fish: the algae it produces feed microorganisms that are toxic to fish. It never rules out other kinds of harm, so saying sewage does not harm fish \"in any way other than\" this one goes too far.",
          "id": "2fda1789-c0e0-44df-85a1-6f053ce8a0b2",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "860a79b6e9eca2bf3d9727f9ee0078a6"
        },
        {
          "body": "The stimulus says the algae give toxic microorganisms \"a rich food source.\" It says nothing about how fast those microorganisms reproduce or how they compare with other microorganisms. Plenty of food for one group doesn't prove that group outgrows all the others.",
          "id": "60b97f01-e22f-43fd-aa71-03aeb36a0cf0",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "282ab6f2109dbf63c4bcba15c732b0b2"
        },
        {
          "body": "This connects the first cause to the final effect: sewage overnutrifies the water, the water grows algae, and the algae sometimes feed microorganisms that kill most of the fish.\n\nThe word \"can\" is what makes it safe. Because the last link only happens \"sometimes,\" the chain proves possibility, not certainty. When a chain has a weak link, the right answer keeps that weakness in its wording.",
          "id": "702c16eb-6f6d-4fcd-8897-8b6b1ade5c68",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "1a6704a3787ba3a80e41933e0cefc066"
        },
        {
          "body": "Sewage pollution is one way to get algae to proliferate. Nothing says it's the only way. D treats a cause as a requirement. Test it: an estuary with no sewage that gets nutrients from farm runoff could still fill with algae, and nothing in the stimulus says otherwise.",
          "id": "d5985aac-6834-4aa5-b3a2-6293ba6f8ccb",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "8cdc637ab63df762273ca62a363a35d2"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "Which one of the following can be properly inferred from the information above?",
      "qid": "PT135-S4-Q6",
      "question_type": "Must Be True",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "Look for a softly worded answer that joins the start of the chain to the end: overnutrifying an estuary with sewage can lead to most of its fish dying. An answer that says this \"will\" happen goes past the \"sometimes\".",
          "id": "130ec879-fc10-40fe-a8a7-4b200e375b3a",
          "label": "Goal",
          "md5": "bc035744a779cc42b8ed60f8155d4cc6",
          "position": 2
        },
        {
          "body": "Coastal estuaries are easily polluted by nutrient-rich sewage.\n\nWhen that pollution overnutrifies the water, algae proliferate.\nsewage overnutrifies water → algae proliferate\n\nThe abundant algae \"sometimes\" feed microorganisms that are toxic to fish, killing most of the fish.\nabundant algae → (sometimes) toxic microorganisms thrive → most fish die",
          "id": "5412f9a3-8035-4b00-b2bf-04a8aa800dbf",
          "label": "Given",
          "md5": "4a1076442d4cce30876f2b3e8191dd59",
          "position": 0
        },
        {
          "body": "Each sentence picks up where the last one ended: sewage leads to overnutrified water, overnutrified water leads to algae, and algae can lead to a fish kill. When causes and effects line up end to end like this, connect the first cause to the last effect.\n\nWatch the word \"sometimes\" on the last link. The algae don't always feed toxic microorganisms. So the chain doesn't prove that sewage always kills the fish. It proves that it can.",
          "id": "6d38891c-244c-42e4-b5dd-2a1a539ca4bd",
          "label": "Evaluate",
          "md5": "00f0307a4210ce0762e0f68bde51499b",
          "position": 1
        }
      ],
      "stimulus": "The calm, shallow waters of coastal estuaries are easily polluted by nutrient-rich sewage. When estuary waters become overnutrified as a result, algae proliferate. The abundant algae, in turn, sometimes provide a rich food source for microorganisms that are toxic to fish, thereby killing most of the fish in the estuary."
    },
    {
      "correct_choice": "E",
      "explanation_id": "bf9db87d-78db-462e-8e1c-df1a59d2c539",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "The author doesn't need to assume that Astorga sought out opinion polls because she has no strong opinions of her own.\n\nShe may well have strong opinions but still seek opinion polls to see how well they align with the strong opinions of voters.\n\nFurthermore, she may have strong opinions of what she thinks the new mayor ought to do, while still hiding those opinions and just telling the voters what they want to hear.\n\nSo negating this, and saying \"she does have at least one strong opinion\" wouldn't weaken the argument at all.",
          "id": "03356cda-4598-4639-8b92-5caadd210428",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "8db0d03e94f614e996b5ed30d0d41e37"
        },
        {
          "body": "The argument is only about whether Astorga's intent matches her current rhetoric. The argument isn't affected one way or the other by whether Astorga wins or loses the election, so the author doesn't need to assume anything about what votes she'll receive or not receive.\n\nFurthermore, the word/concept of \"most\" will be wrong on Necessary Assumption 99.5% of the time we see it. When you negate \"most\" you're basically saying, \"Nuh-uh, author. It's not true that at least 51% of voters are unlikely to be persuaded. It's at most 49%.\"\n\nNo argument is ever badly weakened by switching from \"at least 51%\" to \"at most 49%\", so negating \"most\" will basically never weaken.",
          "id": "d2dc3307-747b-4ada-98a7-3b41e91b220e",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "09b301af66420fb17c4220fe1352f0c1"
        },
        {
          "body": "The author didn't indicate any skepticism of the opinion polls. He may well believe that the opinion polls correctly reflect the voters' actual wishlist, but still believe that Astorga has no plans to actually follow through on these campaign promises (she's just telling voters what they want to hear in order to get elected).",
          "id": "e45417bf-fb72-42e9-a62b-7d8a68ec2316",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "749579ac920b209290d0457de72225b6"
        },
        {
          "body": "This just establishes the difference that the author was assuming.\n\nAstorga is telling voters that she'll do the things she knows they want the mayor to do. The author believes that voters aren't hearing what she'll really do.\n\nSo the author is assuming that she won't really do the things she knows they want the mayor to do.\n\nIf we negated this answer, we'd get, \"Astorga actually does intend, if elected, to do [the things she's currently telling voters that she intends to do]\", which would badly weaken the argument by essentially contradicting the conclusion.",
          "id": "ea4e56d5-7ed5-4995-b09f-c8acd2c342fe",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "90d27f6c36cfd101411b56bc713e4098"
        },
        {
          "body": "The argument is about Intent, not results / capacity / feasibility. The author doesn't have to assume that Astorga is incapable of following through on her campaign promises.\n\nHe might believe that she's perfectly capable of following through on the promises, but just doesn't plan to because she's not willing to pursue those ends.",
          "id": "f3acb51f-6086-4339-8721-c2ee1ee0f7fe",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "6a5cfe43caac20ef3d75cc6c8b00b1b7"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "Which one of the following is a questionable assumption on which the argument relies?",
      "qid": "PT107-S3-Q6",
      "question_type": "Necessary Assumption",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "Astorga is telling voters that, if she becomes mayor, she'll do [all the things she's learned from opinion polls that voters want the new mayor to do].",
          "id": "6baaeaa5-af55-4696-afe9-931300cd245e",
          "label": "Evidence",
          "md5": "df5013624b0c2a015f92cf5f167f3c40",
          "position": 1
        },
        {
          "body": "Voters aren't being told what Astorga really plans to do if she becomes mayor.",
          "id": "b575d6b5-26c9-4589-a8a9-8d86175e140b",
          "label": "Conclusion",
          "md5": "476b3c3017a46d6ca3918139fbc3b3ec",
          "position": 0
        },
        {
          "body": "Look for an answer establishing the \"assumed difference\" -- i.e., Astorga doesn't intend to do the stuff she's currently telling voters that she'll do.\nOr look for an answer that the author is clearly committed to thinking, something that, if negated, would badly weaken his argument.",
          "id": "e0e6a290-a156-4db9-82fd-02c9c5a36db6",
          "label": "Goal",
          "md5": "f59a54681e4443a8644ab93c17408309",
          "position": 3
        },
        {
          "body": "How did the author think he proved that voters aren't really hearing what Astorga plans to do? This is a Comparison argument in which the author is assuming a difference.\nThe author assumes that what Astorga plans to do is different from what she's currently telling voters she'll do.\nShe seems to be cynically thinking that Astorga has her own plans for being mayor, and she is just looking at opinion polls and disingenuously lying to voters, acting like she's going to work on their wishlist, but she's really just saying that stuff to get elected, and then will proceed with her \"real\" agenda.",
          "id": "fcc107b7-f071-425f-a6d4-8e889437ee41",
          "label": "Evaluate",
          "md5": "ff24734237e522e9f615e2c99c6ccecc",
          "position": 2
        }
      ],
      "stimulus": "Astorga’s campaign promises are apparently just an attempt to please voters. What she says she will do if elected mayor is simply what she has learned from opinion polls that voters want the new mayor to do. Therefore, voters are not being told what Astorga actually intends to do if she becomes mayor."
    },
    {
      "correct_choice": "C",
      "explanation_id": "4f16eabb-fdab-47b9-9878-daafaf426e93",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "Jack does not discuss the judiciousness of how something is spread.",
          "id": "62919a91-75a9-4e4a-a9be-f80e2514f675",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "f4f4df2ec8da0ef8f9c7fdbc71e4ac80"
        },
        {
          "body": "Jack mistakes the risk of paying the entire cost of repairs with the risk (or likelihood) of needing insurance.",
          "id": "a5f5cbc0-d797-4553-8a10-a4e1a5431108",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "dedc87c05ada58d9083ae0fd74ef2aeb"
        },
        {
          "body": "The term \"decreases\" is used with the same meaning by both speakers.",
          "id": "abb3dd9e-974e-4e41-bc94-c8accff21c27",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "38c0b4966e440c3c77b6866999ac5bf0"
        },
        {
          "body": "Jack does not discuss other policyholders.",
          "id": "e3a780b4-0648-411d-b430-5428dbcc9c1f",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "4d4f6700668cb34a3e2ffe1df908da24"
        },
        {
          "body": "The term \"fire insurance\" is an example of \"hazard insurance.\"",
          "id": "fe3f20ec-cdcf-4ca1-a0b9-f01739e88bba",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "cbc21594bf6ffb6a872f80a6e8012d1e"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "Jack's response most clearly trades on an ambiguity in which one of the following expressions used by Melinda?",
      "qid": "PT124-S1-Q12",
      "question_type": "Flaw",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "Jack misunderstood Melinda to be claiming that buying insurance lessens one's risk of needing that insurance. Instead the risk that Melinda discusses is that of paying for the entire loss.",
          "id": "85ab9540-103d-4448-a1d1-0150ad324690",
          "label": "Evaluate",
          "md5": "f25efe62f55ad7b0322859fce75962e5",
          "position": 1
        },
        {
          "body": "Melinda: Hazard insurance decreases an individual's risk by judiciously spreading the risk among many policyholders.\nJack: I disagree. It makes sense for me to buy fire insurance for my house, but I don't see how doing so lessens the chances that my house will burn down.",
          "id": "d77cf13b-5137-4d54-90c5-5b82e138981a",
          "label": "Given",
          "md5": "51f8343049ea68daa98e37425ebd49d6",
          "position": 0
        }
      ],
      "stimulus": "Melinda: Hazard insurance decreases an individual's risk by judiciously spreading the risk among many policyholders.\nJack: I disagree. It makes sense for me to buy fire insurance for my house, but I don't see how doing so lessens the chances that my house will burn down."
    },
    {
      "correct_choice": "B",
      "explanation_id": "ac574eb4-f700-4220-91ae-c2fd4783e199",
      "notes": [
        {
          "body": "If most low-melting-point medications are soft-gels, that points M toward a form this company can't make. It gives no reason to think M ends up as a capsule, and it never puts M under the unpleasant-taste rule.",
          "id": "242195a2-95b6-484f-b56e-d57bc5cb503b",
          "letter": "D",
          "md5": "9b29b7a714bdd7fc17115a5ed80bc17f"
        },
        {
          "body": "This is the missing trigger. With M tasting unpleasant, the first premise applies: M will generally come as a tablet, capsule, or soft-gel. The heat rules out tablet, the company's missing technology rules out soft-gel, and capsule is what remains.\nPT → (T or C or SG)\nPT, ~T, ~SG, so C\n\nThe move: when an argument eliminates options from a rule, check that the rule actually applies to the thing in the conclusion.",
          "id": "47fe78f1-cd86-492d-b1cf-bb1b3decbfd2",
          "letter": "B",
          "md5": "a021aa9db386c33ebd78ff19817711b9"
        },
        {
          "body": "If M can be made as a liquid, that adds a fourth option outside tablet, capsule, and soft-gel. That makes capsule less certain, not more. It also does nothing to show that M tastes unpleasant, so the three-option rule still isn't triggered.",
          "id": "63ff81ea-97b3-4c18-979e-d96f1723e02f",
          "letter": "A",
          "md5": "3b2072c08abacf895510d9c672c017ab"
        },
        {
          "body": "This says a capsule medication isn't also a soft-gel (C → ~SG). We already know M won't be a soft-gel, so this adds nothing. And it still doesn't tell us M is limited to the three forms, because nothing says M tastes unpleasant.",
          "id": "d1cd2901-904c-49c5-853a-013b60d5475a",
          "letter": "C",
          "md5": "20e37f1596033e16283b1d615dfadf5e"
        },
        {
          "body": "This gives a reason to choose capsules for bad-tasting medicine, but it only matters if M tastes bad. E never says it does. Without that, M isn't covered by the rule and could be produced in a form outside the three listed.",
          "id": "ef879f8c-59b5-47d1-a3e0-9cfd7646a061",
          "letter": "E",
          "md5": "2f739507c3328718a9f0d318ddd4082f"
        }
      ],
      "prompt": "The conclusion is most strongly supported by the reasoning in the argument if which one of the following is assumed?",
      "qid": "PT132-S2-Q3",
      "question_type": "Strengthen",
      "steps": [
        {
          "body": "Medications with an unpleasant taste are generally produced only in tablet, capsule, or soft-gel form.\nPT → (T or C or SG)\n\nThe active ingredient in medication M is a waxy substance that cannot tolerate the heat used to manufacture tablets because it has a low melting point.\n~T\n\nThe company developing M does not have soft-gel manufacturing technology and manufactures all its medications itself.\n~SG",
          "id": "41eff948-d99b-4ede-b6e1-9d513e0de2f8",
          "label": "Evidence",
          "md5": "41fbdb0fb68260cf91a839602ba59e59",
          "position": 0
        },
        {
          "body": "The stem says \"if which one of the following is assumed\", so we want the piece that makes the conclusion follow. The missing piece is the rule's trigger: M has an unpleasant taste. Once that's in place, the three-option rule applies, two options are gone, and capsule is the one left.",
          "id": "a65bed50-5aa2-42be-9d04-2abc72aa56fd",
          "label": "Goal",
          "md5": "79bf7d390e41204b0242b1abc060d3f9",
          "position": 3
        },
        {
          "body": "M will most likely be produced in capsule form.\nC",
          "id": "bfdc099c-b881-48a9-a793-c558726d8a4a",
          "label": "Conclusion",
          "md5": "b80c1b4cfb978cb10c8fcbcdabfa84f8",
          "position": 1
        },
        {
